//
// PROBE: the `_in` family — words that act on a *named* stack.
//
// For each word that acts on the current stack there is a sibling naming its
// target: `drop`/`drop_in`, `clear`/`clear_in`, `dup_one`/`dup_one_in`,
// `swap`/`swap_in`. The sibling takes the stack name **on top**, which is the
// operand reversal again, and it does not switch stacks — the current stack is
// untouched throughout.
//
// Three of these had the wrong shape until they were compared against the
// oracle, and all three were silent about it because no corpus program calls
// them:
//
//   - `swap_in` ignored its count and exchanged the top two, which is
//     `swap_one`, and left the count behind. Its **name** is on top: the inline wrapper
//     pulls n then name and passes (n, name), but the function it calls reads
//     parameter one as the name (swap.rs:57,90-92), so the two are crossed.
//   - `dup_many_in` pulled the count before the name; the reference pulls the
//     name first (reference/rust_multistack/src/stdlib/dup.rs:71-72), so the
//     source reads `<count> <name> dup_many_in`.
//   - `drop_stack` pulled a stack name. It takes none: it removes the
//     **current** stack outright (drop.rs:54-66, ts_drop_stack.rs:10-21).
//   - `return_to` moved the workbench's top to the current stack, which is
//     `take`. It takes a name (workbench.rs:43-59).
//
:t ensure_stack
@t
1 2 3
@main
"probe: drop_in removes one value from a named stack" println
:t drop_in
@t
debug.display_stack
@main
"probe: dup_one_in and dup_many_in duplicate its top" println
:t dup_one_in
@t
debug.display_stack
@main
2 :t dup_many_in
@t
debug.display_stack
@main
"probe: swap_in reorders inside the named stack" println
1 :t swap_in
@t
debug.display_stack
@main
"probe: clear_in empties it, the current stack untouched" println
:t clear_in
current println
@t
debug.display_stack
@main
"probe: return_to moves the workbench top to a named stack" println
9 .
:t return_to
@t
debug.display_stack
